Great question — this is exactly what judges will ask. Here's how each idea handles low-quality cameras (think ₹8,000 Redmi phones with scratched lenses, poor lighting, 2MP front cameras):

Handling Bad Camera Quality: The "Bharat-Proof" Approach
The Core Problem
Rural Innovation Critic: "Let's be real. A farmer in Azadpur isn't using an iPhone 15 Pro. He's got a 3-year-old phone with a cracked screen, dusty lens, and he's shooting in 5 AM mandi lighting — fluorescent tubes, shadows everywhere, motion blur from the chaos. If your AI breaks on this, you've built a toy for Bangalore, not a tool for Bharat."

Strategy 1: Preprocessing Pipeline (Works for All 3 Ideas)
Before the image hits Bedrock, run a lightweight enhancement pipeline:

Raw Frame → Denoise → Auto-Exposure → Sharpen → Super-Resolution → Bedrock
Step	AWS Service	What It Does
Denoise	Lambda + OpenCV	Removes grain from low-light shots
Auto-Exposure	Lambda + PIL/OpenCV	Normalizes brightness, fixes backlit scenes
Sharpen	Lambda + OpenCV	Recovers edge detail from motion blur
Super-Resolution	SageMaker Endpoint (Real-ESRGAN or similar)	Upscales 480p → 1080p, recovers detail
Adaptive Compression	Lambda	Sends optimal resolution to Bedrock (saves cost + latency)
Cost: ~$0.002 per image, adds ~300ms latency. Worth it.

Strategy 2: Idea-Specific Fallbacks
MandiBrain (Mandi Auction)
Problem	Solution
Can't read hand signals clearly	Fallback to audio analysis — the AI listens to the auctioneer's voice, extracts numbers using Transcribe. Hand signals become secondary confirmation, not primary input.
Too much motion blur	Burst mode — capture 5 frames/second, pick the sharpest one using Laplacian variance scoring
Poor lighting at 5 AM	Prompt the user — "Flash chalu karo" (turn on flash) via voice. Or use phone's torch as continuous light.
Trader faces not visible	Doesn't matter — we track traders by position + clothing color, not faces. Works even with backs turned.
Graceful Degradation:

Full Vision Mode → Audio-Assisted Mode → Manual Input Mode
     (best)            (medium)              (fallback)
If vision confidence < 60%, the AI says: "Bhai, camera saaf karo ya rate khud bolo"

ShramShakti (Construction Site)
Problem	Solution
CCTV is old/grainy	Temporal aggregation — instead of single-frame detection, analyze 10 seconds of video. A worker missed in frame 1 will appear in frame 5. Confidence builds over time.
Workers are far from camera	Zone-based counting — divide site into grid zones. Count workers per zone. Even blurry blobs can be counted if they move.
Helmet detection fails	Color-based fallback — Indian safety helmets are legally required to be yellow/white. If shape detection fails, fall back to color blob detection.
Night shift / poor lighting	Infrared compatibility — most construction CCTVs have IR mode. Train model on IR images too.
